{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yang/antlr/packet.cljc | sed -n '1,520p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yang.antlr.packet\n     2\t  \"The target-neutral parse-tree IR and its reader (docs/design/yang.antlr.md\n     3\t   §5.5). ANTLR has no neutral tree: each target materializes its own\n     4\t   parse-tree objects. This namespace owns the neutral form every host's\n     5\t   exporter produces (`yang.antlr.cst`, one file per host) and every\n     6\t   language's CST-to-UAST converter consumes: the CST packet, version 1.\n     7\t\n     8\t   Packet (version 1):\n     9\t\n    10\t     {:yang.cst/version 1\n    11\t      :yang.cst/unit u\n    12\t      :yang.cst/grammar id\n    13\t      :yang.cst/outcome :yang.cst/ok | :yang.cst/syntax-error | :yang.cst/source-error\n    14\t      :yang.cst/root 0                       ; ok only\n    15\t      :yang.cst/nodes [record ...]           ; ok only, preorder, id = index\n    16\t      :yang.cst/errors [error ...]           ; non-ok only\n    17\t      :yang.cst/complete true}\n    18\t\n    19\t   Rule record  {:id n :kind :rule :rule \\\"name\\\" :children [ids] :span [s e]}\n    20\t   Token record {:id n :kind :token :type \\\"NAME\\\" :text t :span [s e]}\n    21\t                 plus :synthetic true when the token's text is not the source\n    22\t                 at its span (indentation helpers, EOF); its span is then\n    23\t                 zero-width.\n    24\t   Error record {:kind :syntax-error :message m :line l :column c :span [s e]}\n    25\t\n    26\t   Spans are half-open UTF-8 byte offsets into the sealed source. A unit with\n    27\t   any syntax error yields an error packet and no nodes: never a partial\n    28\t   program.\n    29\t\n    30\t   Invariants every target's exporter must meet (`validate!` checks 1, 6 and\n    31\t   7 structurally, and 2-5 against the source when it is given; `conform` is\n    32\t   the source-aware check as a vector of violations):\n    33\t\n    34\t     1. ids are preorder indexes; :root is 0; child edges form a tree\n    35\t        (`validate!`).\n    36\t     2. :rule is the grammar rule name from the parser's rule-name table,\n    37\t        never a target class name; :type is the symbolic token name, else the\n    38\t        literal name, else the decimal type, \\\"EOF\\\" for EOF.\n    39\t     3. :span is half-open UTF-8 byte offsets into the sealed source. Each\n    40\t        target converts its own index unit at the boundary (the JVM indexes\n    41\t        code points; the JS and Dart streams are opened in code-point mode).\n    42\t     4. A token is :synthetic with a zero-width span and the canonical helper\n    43\t        text of its type exactly when `synthetic-policy` says so: DEDENT and\n    44\t        EOF always, NEWLINE only at the end of the source, no other type ever.\n    45\t        A real token's text is the source at its span.\n    46\t     5. A rule's span covers its real children only; an empty rule is\n    47\t        zero-width.\n    48\t     6. Any syntax error yields an error packet with :yang.cst/errors and no\n    49\t        nodes.\n    50\t     7. :yang.cst/grammar names the grammar revision and tool; the target does\n    51\t        not appear in the packet. Target equivalence is the contract.\n    52\t\n    53\t   Portable and language-agnostic: child edges are data, so the tree is\n    54\t   validated before anything walks it.\")\n    55\t\n    56\t\n    57\t(def packet-version 1)\n    58\t\n    59\t\n    60\t;; =============================================================================\n    61\t;; Source text\n    62\t;; =============================================================================\n    63\t\n    64\t(defn code-points\n    65\t  \"The code points of `s` as a vector of integers.\"\n    66\t  [s]\n    67\t  #?(:cljd (vec (.-runes ^String s))\n    68\t     :clj (mapv long (.toArray (.codePoints ^String s)))\n    69\t     :cljs (mapv #(.codePointAt % 0) (js/Array.from s))))\n    70\t\n    71\t\n    72\t(defn utf8-length\n    73\t  \"The UTF-8 byte count of one code point.\"\n    74\t  [cp]\n    75\t  (cond (< cp 0x80) 1\n    76\t        (< cp 0x800) 2\n    77\t        (< cp 0x10000) 3\n    78\t        :else 4))\n    79\t\n    80\t\n    81\t(defn utf8-bytes\n    82\t  \"The UTF-8 byte count of a string: span ends are checked against it.\"\n    83\t  [s]\n    84\t  (reduce + 0 (map utf8-length (code-points s))))\n    85\t\n    86\t\n    87\t(defn byte-offsets\n    88\t  \"Byte offset of each code point index of `cps`, plus the total at index n.\"\n    89\t  [cps]\n    90\t  (loop [out [0]\n    91\t         acc 0\n    92\t         cps (seq cps)]\n    93\t    (if cps\n    94\t      (let [acc (+ acc (utf8-length (first cps)))]\n    95\t        (recur (conj out acc) acc (next cps)))\n    96\t      out)))\n    97\t\n    98\t\n    99\t(def synthetic-policy\n   100\t  \"Which tokens an indentation helper may fabricate, keyed on the token's\n   101\t   type: `:when` is `:always` (every token of the type is synthetic) or\n   102\t   `:at-end` (synthetic only at the end of the source, real elsewhere), and\n   103\t   `:text` is the canonical text a synthetic token carries. A type absent\n   104\t   from the map is never synthetic.\"\n   105\t  {\"DEDENT\" {:when :always, :text \"\"},\n   106\t   \"EOF\" {:when :always, :text \"<EOF>\"},\n   107\t   \"NEWLINE\" {:when :at-end, :text \"\\n\"}})\n   108\t\n   109\t\n   110\t;; =============================================================================\n   111\t;; Packet construction and the parser stage transform\n   112\t;; =============================================================================\n   113\t\n   114\t(defn base\n   115\t  [grammar-id unit]\n   116\t  {:yang.cst/version packet-version,\n   117\t   :yang.cst/unit unit,\n   118\t   :yang.cst/grammar grammar-id,\n   119\t   :yang.cst/complete true})\n   120\t\n   121\t\n   122\t(defn source-error\n   123\t  [grammar-id unit message data]\n   124\t  (assoc (base grammar-id unit)\n   125\t         :yang.cst/outcome :yang.cst/source-error\n   126\t         :yang.cst/errors [(merge {:kind :source-error, :message message}\n   127\t                                  data)]))\n   128\t\n   129\t\n   130\t(defn transform\n   131\t  \"The parser stage's transform over source events: chunks accumulate per\n   132\t   unit by ordinal; a seal with every chunk present assembles the snapshot,\n   133\t   parses it with `parse-unit` (fn [worker unit source] packet), and emits\n   134\t   one packet on port `:cst`. A conflicting duplicate chunk, a seal with\n   135\t   chunks missing, or an unknown event emits a source-error packet instead.\n   136\t   State is `{unit {ordinal text}}`.\"\n   137\t  [parse-unit worker]\n   138\t  (let [grammar-id (get-in worker [:profile :grammar-id])]\n   139\t    (fn [units event]\n   140\t      (let [unit (:yang.source/unit event)]\n   141\t        (case (:yang.source/event event)\n   142\t          :yang.source/chunk\n   143\t          (let [ordinal (:yang.source/ordinal event)\n   144\t                text (:yang.source/text event)\n   145\t                prior (get-in units [unit ordinal])]\n   146\t            (if (and (some? prior) (not= prior text))\n   147\t              [(dissoc units unit)\n   148\t               [[:cst (source-error grammar-id unit \"Conflicting duplicate chunk\"\n   149\t                                    {:ordinal ordinal})]]]\n   150\t              [(assoc-in units [unit ordinal] text) []]))\n   151\t\n   152\t          :yang.source/seal\n   153\t          (let [chunks (get units unit {})\n   154\t                n (:yang.source/chunk-count event)]\n   155\t            (if (= (set (range n)) (set (keys chunks)))\n   156\t              [(dissoc units unit)\n   157\t               [[:cst (parse-unit worker unit\n   158\t                                  (apply str (map chunks (range n))))]]]\n   159\t              [(dissoc units unit)\n   160\t               [[:cst (source-error grammar-id unit \"Seal with missing chunks\"\n   161\t                                    {:chunk-count n,\n   162\t                                     :present (vec (sort (keys chunks)))})]]]))\n   163\t\n   164\t          [units\n   165\t           [[:cst (source-error grammar-id unit \"Unknown source event\"\n   166\t                                {:event (:yang.source/event event)})]]])))))\n   167\t\n   168\t\n   169\t(defn- malformed!\n   170\t  [message data]\n   171\t  (throw (ex-info message\n   172\t                  (merge {:yang.python.antlr/diagnostic\n   173\t                          :yang.python.antlr/malformed-cst}\n   174\t                         data))))\n   175\t\n   176\t\n   177\t;; =============================================================================\n   178\t;; Validation: the packet's contract as a vector of violations\n   179\t;; =============================================================================\n   180\t\n   181\t(defn- span?\n   182\t  [s]\n   183\t  (and (vector? s)\n   184\t       (= 2 (count s))\n   185\t       (integer? (first s))\n   186\t       (integer? (second s))\n   187\t       (<= 0 (first s) (second s))))\n   188\t\n   189\t\n   190\t(defn- blank-string?\n   191\t  [s]\n   192\t  (or (not (string? s)) (= \"\" s)))\n   193\t\n   194\t\n   195\t(defn- metadata-violations\n   196\t  \"The packet's own fields: version, unit, grammar name, completion, outcome,\n   197\t   and the outcome-specific contract (an ok packet has a root and nodes and\n   198\t   no errors; every other outcome has errors and neither nodes nor a root).\"\n   199\t  [packet]\n   200\t  (let [outcome (:yang.cst/outcome packet)\n   201\t        errors (:yang.cst/errors packet)\n   202\t        note (fn [why] {:violation why})]\n   203\t    (cond-> []\n   204\t      (not= packet-version (:yang.cst/version packet))\n   205\t      (conj (note :bad-version))\n   206\t      (not (contains? packet :yang.cst/unit))\n   207\t      (conj (note :no-unit))\n   208\t      (blank-string? (:yang.cst/grammar packet))\n   209\t      (conj (note :bad-grammar-name))\n   210\t      (not (true? (:yang.cst/complete packet)))\n   211\t      (conj (note :not-complete))\n   212\t      (not (contains? #{:yang.cst/ok :yang.cst/syntax-error :yang.cst/source-error}\n   213\t                      outcome))\n   214\t      (conj (note :unknown-outcome))\n   215\t      (and (= :yang.cst/ok outcome) (contains? packet :yang.cst/errors))\n   216\t      (conj (note :ok-packet-has-errors))\n   217\t      (and (= :yang.cst/ok outcome) (not= 0 (:yang.cst/root packet)))\n   218\t      (conj (note :no-root))\n   219\t      (and (not= :yang.cst/ok outcome)\n   220\t           (or (contains? packet :yang.cst/nodes)\n   221\t               (contains? packet :yang.cst/root)))\n   222\t      (conj (note :error-packet-has-nodes))\n   223\t      (and (contains? #{:yang.cst/syntax-error :yang.cst/source-error} outcome)\n   224\t           (not (and (vector? errors) (seq errors))))\n   225\t      (conj (note :no-errors)))))\n   226\t\n   227\t\n   228\t(defn- error-violations\n   229\t  \"Each error record of a non-ok packet: its kind matches the outcome, it has\n   230\t   a message, a syntax error carries ANTLR's line and column as captured, and\n   231\t   a span (when present) is a byte span.\"\n   232\t  [packet]\n   233\t  (let [kind ({:yang.cst/syntax-error :syntax-error,\n   234\t               :yang.cst/source-error :source-error}\n   235\t              (:yang.cst/outcome packet))\n   236\t        bad (volatile! [])]\n   237\t    (doseq [[i e] (map-indexed vector (:yang.cst/errors packet))\n   238\t            :let [note! (fn [why] (vswap! bad conj {:error i, :violation why}))]]\n   239\t      (when (not= kind (:kind e)) (note! :error-kind))\n   240\t      (when-not (string? (:message e)) (note! :error-message))\n   241\t      (when (and (= :syntax-error kind)\n   242\t                 (not (and (integer? (:line e)) (<= 1 (:line e)))))\n   243\t        (note! :error-line))\n   244\t      (when (and (= :syntax-error kind)\n   245\t                 (not (and (integer? (:column e)) (<= 0 (:column e)))))\n   246\t        (note! :error-column))\n   247\t      (when (and (some? (:span e)) (not (span? (:span e))))\n   248\t        (note! :error-span)))\n   249\t    @bad))\n   250\t\n   251\t\n   252\t(defn- node-field-violations\n   253\t  [nodes rule-names]\n   254\t  (let [bad (volatile! [])\n   255\t        note! (fn [id why] (vswap! bad conj {:id id, :violation why}))]\n   256\t    (doseq [i (range (count nodes))\n   257\t            :let [n (nth nodes i)]]\n   258\t      (cond\n   259\t        (not= i (:id n)) (note! i :id-not-index)\n   260\t        (= :rule (:kind n))\n   261\t        (do (when (blank-string? (:rule n)) (note! i :bad-rule-name))\n   262\t            (when (and rule-names\n   263\t                       (string? (:rule n))\n   264\t                       (not (contains? rule-names (:rule n))))\n   265\t              (note! i :unknown-rule-name))\n   266\t            (when-not (vector? (:children n)) (note! i :bad-children))\n   267\t            (when-not (span? (:span n)) (note! i :bad-span)))\n   268\t        (= :token (:kind n))\n   269\t        (do (when (blank-string? (:type n)) (note! i :bad-token-type))\n   270\t            (when-not (string? (:text n)) (note! i :bad-token-text))\n   271\t            (when (seq (:children n)) (note! i :token-has-children))\n   272\t            (when-not (span? (:span n)) (note! i :bad-span)))\n   273\t        :else (note! i :unknown-kind)))\n   274\t    @bad))\n   275\t\n   276\t\n   277\t(defn- edge-violations\n   278\t  \"One preorder walk from the root: a rule's children are exactly the roots of\n   279\t   consecutive subtrees, so every node is reached once, in document order,\n   280\t   and a node outside the walk is unreachable.\"\n   281\t  [nodes]\n   282\t  (let [total (count nodes)\n   283\t        bad (volatile! [])\n   284\t        note! (fn [id why] (vswap! bad conj {:id id, :violation why}))\n   285\t        walk (fn walk\n   286\t               [id]\n   287\t               (let [n (nth nodes id)]\n   288\t                 (if (= :rule (:kind n))\n   289\t                   (loop [cursor (inc id)\n   290\t                          kids (seq (:children n))]\n   291\t                     (if-not kids\n   292\t                       cursor\n   293\t                       (let [k (first kids)]\n   294\t                         (cond\n   295\t                           (not (and (integer? k) (< id k total)))\n   296\t                           (do (note! id :child-edge-not-forward) nil)\n   297\t                           (< k cursor) (do (note! id :duplicate-child-edge) nil)\n   298\t                           (> k cursor) (do (note! id :children-not-preorder) nil)\n   299\t                           :else (when-let [next-id (walk k)]\n   300\t                                   (recur next-id (next kids)))))))\n   301\t                   (inc id))))\n   302\t        end (walk 0)]\n   303\t    (when (and (empty? @bad) (not= end total))\n   304\t      (note! 0 :unreachable-nodes))\n   305\t    @bad))\n   306\t\n   307\t\n   308\t(defn- structure-violations\n   309\t  [packet rule-names]\n   310\t  (let [meta (metadata-violations packet)]\n   311\t    (cond\n   312\t      (seq meta) meta\n   313\t      (= :yang.cst/ok (:yang.cst/outcome packet))\n   314\t      (let [nodes (:yang.cst/nodes packet)]\n   315\t        (if-not (and (vector? nodes) (pos? (count nodes)))\n   316\t          [{:violation :no-nodes}]\n   317\t          (let [fields (node-field-violations nodes rule-names)]\n   318\t            (if (seq fields) fields (edge-violations nodes)))))\n   319\t      :else (error-violations packet))))\n   320\t\n   321\t\n   322\t(defn- source-index\n   323\t  [source]\n   324\t  (let [cps (code-points source)\n   325\t        offsets (byte-offsets cps)]\n   326\t    {:cps cps, :total (peek offsets), :at (zipmap offsets (range))}))\n   327\t\n   328\t\n   329\t(defn- span-violation\n   330\t  \"Why `span` is not a byte span of the source, or nil: it must lie in\n   331\t   [0, total] and begin and end on a UTF-8 character boundary.\"\n   332\t  [{:keys [total at]} [a b]]\n   333\t  (cond\n   334\t    (not (<= 0 a b total)) :span-out-of-range\n   335\t    (not (and (contains? at a) (contains? at b))) :span-not-on-utf8-boundary))\n   336\t\n   337\t\n   338\t(defn- token-violation\n   339\t  \"Why a token record disagrees with the source, or nil. A real token's text\n   340\t   is the source at its span and its type may not be one the policy always\n   341\t   synthesizes; a synthetic token is permitted by the policy for its type, is\n   342\t   zero-width, sits at the end of the source when the policy says so, and\n   343\t   carries the canonical text.\"\n   344\t  [{:keys [cps total at]} n]\n   345\t  (let [[a b] (:span n)\n   346\t        policy (get synthetic-policy (:type n))]\n   347\t    (if (:synthetic n)\n   348\t      (cond\n   349\t        (nil? policy) :synthetic-not-permitted\n   350\t        (not= a b) :synthetic-not-zero-width\n   351\t        (and (= :at-end (:when policy)) (not= a total)) :synthetic-not-at-end\n   352\t        (not= (:text policy) (:text n)) :synthetic-text-not-canonical)\n   353\t      (cond\n   354\t        (= :always (:when policy)) :real-token-of-synthetic-type\n   355\t        (not= (subvec cps (at a) (at b)) (code-points (:text n)))\n   356\t        :token-text-not-source))))\n   357\t\n   358\t\n   359\t(defn- source-violations\n   360\t  \"Invariants 3-5 against `source`: spans are byte spans on character\n   361\t   boundaries, tokens are in document order, token text and synthetic tokens\n   362\t   follow the policy, and a rule's span covers its real children.\"\n   363\t  [packet source]\n   364\t  (let [idx (source-index source)]\n   365\t    (if-not (= :yang.cst/ok (:yang.cst/outcome packet))\n   366\t      (into []\n   367\t            (keep-indexed\n   368\t              (fn [i e]\n   369\t                (when-let [why (and (:span e) (span-violation idx (:span e)))]\n   370\t                  {:error i, :violation why})))\n   371\t            (:yang.cst/errors packet))\n   372\t      (let [nodes (:yang.cst/nodes packet)\n   373\t            bad (volatile! [])\n   374\t            note! (fn [id why] (vswap! bad conj {:id id, :violation why}))\n   375\t            last-end (volatile! 0)]\n   376\t        (doseq [n nodes\n   377\t                :let [id (:id n)\n   378\t                      [a b] (:span n)\n   379\t                      span-why (span-violation idx [a b])]]\n   380\t          (if span-why\n   381\t            (note! id span-why)\n   382\t            (when (= :token (:kind n))\n   383\t              (when-let [why (token-violation idx n)] (note! id why))\n   384\t              (when (< a @last-end) (note! id :token-overlaps-previous))\n   385\t              (when (and (not (:synthetic n)) (< a b)) (vreset! last-end b)))))\n   386\t        (doseq [n nodes\n   387\t                :when (= :rule (:kind n))\n   388\t                :let [real (keep (fn [k]\n   389\t                                   (let [c (nth nodes k)\n   390\t                                         [a b] (:span c)]\n   391\t                                     (when (and (not (:synthetic c)) (< a b))\n   392\t                                       [a b])))\n   393\t                                 (:children n))\n   394\t                      [a b] (:span n)]]\n   395\t          (when (and (seq real)\n   396\t                     (not= [a b] [(apply min (map first real))\n   397\t                                  (apply max (map second real))]))\n   398\t            (note! (:id n) :rule-span-not-children))\n   399\t          (when (and (empty? real) (not= a b))\n   400\t            (note! (:id n) :empty-rule-not-zero-width)))\n   401\t        @bad))))\n   402\t\n   403\t\n   404\t(defn violations\n   405\t  \"Every way `packet` breaks the contract, as a vector of\n   406\t   {:violation kw ...} (empty when it conforms). The structure is checked\n   407\t   first; the source-dependent checks run only on a well-formed packet and\n   408\t   only when `:source` is given. `opts`: `:source` the sealed source text,\n   409\t   `:rule-names` the grammar's rule-name set.\"\n   410\t  [packet {:keys [source rule-names]}]\n   411\t  (let [structural (structure-violations packet rule-names)]\n   412\t    (if (or (seq structural) (nil? source))\n   413\t      structural\n   414\t      (source-violations packet source))))\n   415\t\n   416\t\n   417\t(defn validate!\n   418\t  \"Return the packet when it keeps the contract (`violations`); otherwise\n   419\t   throw a qualified malformed-cst error carrying the first violation and\n   420\t   all of them. Without `opts` the check is structural; with `:source` it is\n   421\t   source-aware.\"\n   422\t  ([packet] (validate! packet nil))\n   423\t  ([packet opts]\n   424\t   (let [bad (violations packet opts)]\n   425\t     (when (seq bad)\n   426\t       (malformed! (str \"CST packet violates its contract: \"\n   427\t                        (name (:violation (first bad))))\n   428\t                   (assoc (select-keys (first bad) [:id :error])\n   429\t                          :violations bad)))\n   430\t     packet)))\n   431\t\n   432\t\n   433\t(defn node\n   434\t  [packet id]\n   435\t  (nth (:yang.cst/nodes packet) id))\n   436\t\n   437\t\n   438\t(defn root\n   439\t  [packet]\n   440\t  (node packet (:yang.cst/root packet)))\n   441\t\n   442\t\n   443\t(defn children\n   444\t  [packet n]\n   445\t  (mapv #(node packet %) (:children n)))\n   446\t\n   447\t\n   448\t(defn rule?\n   449\t  ([n] (= :rule (:kind n)))\n   450\t  ([n rule-name] (and (= :rule (:kind n)) (= rule-name (:rule n)))))\n   451\t\n   452\t\n   453\t(defn token?\n   454\t  ([n] (= :token (:kind n)))\n   455\t  ([n text] (and (= :token (:kind n)) (= text (:text n)))))\n   456\t\n   457\t\n   458\t(defn child-rules\n   459\t  \"The rule children of `n` named `rule-name`, in order.\"\n   460\t  [packet n rule-name]\n   461\t  (filterv #(rule? % rule-name) (children packet n)))\n   462\t\n   463\t\n   464\t(defn has-token?\n   465\t  [packet n text]\n   466\t  (boolean (some #(token? % text) (children packet n))))\n   467\t\n   468\t\n   469\t;; =============================================================================\n   470\t;; Conformance, the per-target oracle\n   471\t;; =============================================================================\n   472\t\n   473\t(defn conform\n   474\t  \"Check a packet against its `source` text; return a vector of violations\n   475\t   (empty when it conforms): the structure, then spans on UTF-8 boundaries,\n   476\t   token text equal to the source, synthetic tokens only where the policy\n   477\t   allows, ordered terminals and rule extents over real children.\"\n   478\t  ([packet source] (conform packet source nil))\n   479\t  ([packet source opts] (violations packet (assoc opts :source source))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
